---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Get an ID & Phone — Base Impact" description="Step-by-step guide to getting a Florida ID, Social Security card, and a Lifeline phone — including for people who are homeless or without a permanent address.">
  <div class="max-w-3xl space-y-8 text-muted leading-relaxed">
    <div>
      <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-semibold text-body mb-3">Get an ID & Phone</h1>
      <p class="text-muted">A step-by-step guide to getting a Florida ID card, Social Security card, and a Lifeline phone — including what to do if you are homeless or don't have a permanent address.</p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-faint">This guide is for information only. Requirements change, so always confirm with the agency before you go. Last reviewed November 2026.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Quick start -->
    <section class="rounded-2xl bg-card p-6">
      <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-body mb-3">Start here — what you need</h2>
      <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-inset p-4">
          <h3 class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-body mb-1">1. Proof of identity</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">Birth certificate, passport, or naturalization certificate.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-inset p-4">
          <h3 class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-body mb-1">2. Proof of SSN</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">Social Security card, W-2, pay stub, or SSA-1099.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-inset p-4">
          <h3 class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-body mb-1">3. Proof of address</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">Two documents with your Florida address — or a shelter letter if homeless.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Florida ID -->
    <section>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-body mb-3">Getting a Florida ID card</h2>
      <p class="mb-3">Florida driver licenses and ID cards are issued by the <strong>Florida Department of Highway Safety and Motor Vehicles (FLHSMV)</strong>. You must visit an office in person for your first ID or if your information has changed.</p>

      <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-body mb-2">Documents to bring (U.S. citizens)</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Primary identification</strong> — one of: certified birth certificate (not a hospital certificate), valid U.S. passport, Consular Report of Birth Abroad, Certificate of Naturalization, or Certificate of Citizenship.</li>
        <li><strong>Proof of Social Security number</strong> — one of: Social Security card, W-2 form (not handwritten), pay stub, SSA-1099, or any 1099 (not handwritten).</li>
        <li><strong>Two proofs of Florida residential address</strong> — such as: deed, mortgage statement, lease agreement, Florida voter registration card, vehicle registration or title, utility bill (dated within 60 days), or automobile payment booklet (dated within 60 days).</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-faint">If your name has changed (marriage, divorce, court order), bring the marriage certificate, divorce decree, or court order. Update your name with the Social Security Administration first — allow 24–48 hours before applying.</p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm"><a href="https://www.flhsmv.gov/driver-licenses-id-cards/what-to-bring/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="text-accent underline">Official FLHSMV "What to Bring" page →</a></p>
    </section>

    <!-- Homeless / no address -->
    <section class="rounded-2xl bg-card p-6">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-body mb-3">If you are homeless or have no permanent address</h2>
      <p class="mb-3">This is the part that stops most people — but there is a path. Florida does not have a separate "homeless ID" process, but you can use a shelter or service provider address in most cases.</p>

      <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-body mb-2">What works as an address</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>A shelter address</strong> — many shelters allow residents to use the shelter's address for mail and ID purposes. Ask the shelter director or case manager for a letter on official letterhead confirming you are a resident.</li>
        <li><strong>A letter from a service provider</strong> — a social worker, case manager, or outreach worker can write a letter confirming where you receive services. It should be on agency letterhead, dated, and include their contact information.</li>
        <li><strong>A Certification of Address form</strong> — FLHSMV form HSMV 71120 can be used in some cases. Ask the tax collector's office or license bureau if it applies to you.</li>
        <li><strong>A PO box or mail drop</strong> — some mail receiving services provide a street address (not a PO box number), which may be accepted.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-body mb-2 mt-4">The "90-day letter"</h3>
      <p class="mb-2">Some shelters and programs provide a letter confirming you have been a resident for a certain period (often 90 days). This is used when a program requires proof of residency duration. Ask your shelter or case manager:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li>"Can you write a letter on agency letterhead confirming my residency?"</li>
        <li>"Does the letter need to state a specific number of days?"</li>
        <li>"Can you include your phone number so the agency can verify it?"</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-faint">A good letter includes: your name, the shelter's full address, your move-in date, the current date, and a staff signature and phone number.</p>

      <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-body mb-2 mt-4">Who can help</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>IDignity</strong> — free help obtaining Florida ID, birth certificates, and Social Security cards. Orlando: 425 N Magnolia Ave, 407-792-1374. <a href="https://idignity.org/get-help/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="text-accent underline">idignity.org/get-help</a></li>
        <li><strong>Your local shelter or case manager</strong> — they often have relationships with the license bureau and can advocate for you.</li>
        <li><strong>211</strong> — call 211 to find ID assistance programs in your county.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Social Security card -->
    <section>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-body mb-3">Getting a Social Security card</h2>
      <p class="mb-3">You need a Social Security card (or proof of your SSN) to get a Florida ID, apply for benefits, and apply for Lifeline. The <strong>Social Security Administration (SSA)</strong> issues replacement cards for free.</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>What to bring</strong> — proof of identity (U.S. passport, driver license, or state ID) and proof of citizenship or lawful status. If you're replacing a card and have a driver license or ID, that's usually enough.</li>
        <li><strong>How to apply</strong> — fill out Form SS-5 (available at ssa.gov) and bring it to your local SSA office. Many offices accept walk-ins; some require appointments.</li>
        <li><strong>Cost</strong> — free. There is no charge for a replacement Social Security card.</li>
        <li><strong>Homeless applicants</strong> — you can use a shelter address or a letter from a service provider as your mailing address. Tell the SSA office if you are homeless so they can help.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm"><a href="https://www.ssa.gov/number-card/request" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="text-accent underline">Request a replacement card at ssa.gov →</a> · <a href="https://www.ssa.gov" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="text-accent underline">ssa.gov</a> · Phone: 1-800-772-1213</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Lifeline phone -->
    <section>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-body mb-3">Getting a Lifeline phone</h2>
      <p class="mb-3">Lifeline is a federal program that gives up to <strong>$9.25/month off phone or internet service</strong> (up to $34.25 on Tribal lands). It is not a free phone — it is a discount on a monthly bill. Many providers offer low-cost plans that become $0 after the discount.</p>

      <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-body mb-2">Who qualifies</h3>
      <p class="mb-2">You qualify if your household income is at or below 135% of the federal poverty level, OR if you participate in any of these:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
        <li>SNAP (food stamps)</li>
        <li>Medicaid</li>
        <li>Supplemental Security Income (SSI)</li>
        <li>Federal Public Housing Assistance (Section 8)</li>
        <li>Veterans Pension or Survivors Benefit</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-body mb-2 mt-4">How to apply</h3>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Apply online</strong> at <a href="https://www.lifelinesupport.org/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="text-accent underline">lifelinesupport.org</a> through the National Verifier — it takes about 10 minutes.</li>
        <li><strong>Or apply by phone</strong> — call the Lifeline Support Center at <a href="tel:18002349473" class="text-accent underline">1-800-234-9473</a> (press 1 for English, 2 for Spanish), 7 days a week, 9 AM – 9 PM ET.</li>
        <li><strong>Or apply through a provider</strong> — companies like SafeLink, Assurance Wireless, and Q Link can submit the application with you. Ask about their Lifeline plans.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-body mb-2 mt-4">What you need to provide</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Proof of identity</strong> — a driver license, state ID, passport, or other government-issued ID.</li>
        <li><strong>Proof of eligibility</strong> — either a document showing your participation in SNAP/Medicaid/SSI/etc., or proof of income (pay stubs, tax return, etc.).</li>
        <li><strong>Proof of address</strong> — if you have one. If you are homeless, a shelter address or letter from a service provider is usually accepted. Tell the National Verifier if you are homeless.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-body mb-2 mt-4">If you are homeless or have no address</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li>Use a shelter address or a letter from a case manager as your mailing address.</li>
        <li>Call the Lifeline Support Center at <a href="tel:18002349473" class="text-accent underline">1-800-234-9473</a> and explain your situation — they can tell you exactly what will be accepted.</li>
        <li>If you have no ID at all, apply for a Florida ID first (see above). Some providers can start the Lifeline application while your ID is pending.</li>
        <li>Survivors of domestic violence or human trafficking may qualify for emergency Lifeline support for up to 6 months under the Safe Connections Act. Visit <a href="https://www.lifelinesupport.org/survivor-benefit" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="text-accent underline">lifelinesupport.org/survivor-benefit</a>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm"><a href="https://www.lifelinesupport.org/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="text-accent underline">lifelinesupport.org →</a> · <a href="https://www.fcc.gov/lifeline-consumers" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="text-accent underline">FCC Lifeline page →</a></p>
    </section>

    <!-- Order of operations -->
    <section class="rounded-2xl bg-card p-6">
      <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-body mb-3">The order that works best</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Social Security card first</strong> — it is free and fast, and you need it for almost everything else. Bring proof of identity and go to your local SSA office.</li>
        <li><strong>Florida ID card second</strong> — bring your birth certificate, Social Security card, and two proofs of address (or a shelter letter). Apply at your county tax collector's office or license bureau.</li>
        <li><strong>Lifeline phone third</strong> — once you have an ID and an address (even a shelter address), apply at lifelinesupport.org or through a provider.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-faint">If you are working with a case manager or shelter, tell them your plan. They can often make calls, write letters, and walk you through each step.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Help -->
    <section class="rounded-2xl bg-band p-6 text-on-fill">
      <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold mb-2">Need help with any of this?</h2>
      <p class="text-sm opacity-90 mb-4">Base Impact can help you find a navigator or connect you with a program that walks you through the process.</p>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a href="/contact" class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center rounded-lg bg-fill px-5 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-on-fill hover:bg-fill-hover">Contact us</a>
        <a href="/directory" class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center rounded-lg border border-line bg-card/20 px-5 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-on-fill hover:bg-card/40">Find resources</a>
        <a href="tel:211" class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center rounded-lg border border-line bg-card/20 px-5 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-on-fill hover:bg-card/40">Call 211</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
